---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import GovernedAITransaction from "../components/diagrams/GovernedAITransaction.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How It Works | BeaconGuard Governed AI Transaction"
  description="See how BeaconGuard governs the release, authorizes provider invocation, treats AI results as untrusted proposals, authorizes the declared enterprise action, and records decisions for verification."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>How It Works</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A governed AI transaction spans release governance, request admission,
      provider invocation, an untrusted AI-generated result, independent
      authorization of the declared enterprise action, and verifiable decision
      evidence. BeaconGuard authorizes; the enterprise application executes;
      the system of record remains authoritative.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Complete governed AI transaction</h2>
    <p>
      Reaching the AI provider is not the final governed outcome. Enterprise
      effect requires independent authorization of the declared intended action.
      Provider success is not enterprise authorization.
    </p>
    <GovernedAITransaction />
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>1. Govern the release</h2>
    <p>
      Compliance Playbooks are validated, approved, signed, distributed, and
      activated. Only an Active Verified Release governs later authorization
      decisions. Distribution alone does not activate a release.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>2. Authorize the request</h2>
    <p>
      Request Admission Authorization decides whether the enterprise application
      may invoke the AI provider under the Active Verified Release. Outcomes are
      binary: <strong>ALLOW</strong> or <strong>DENY</strong>. DENY records the
      decision and blocks provider invocation.
    </p>
    <p class="muted-copy">
      Where admission enforcement is configured, the technical subflow may still
      include Validate → Verify → Evaluate → Enforce → Record: normalize and
      validate context, verify trust and freshness, evaluate against the Active
      Verified Release, enforce ALLOW or DENY, and persist a Verifiable Decision
      Record before an ALLOW may permit provider invocation for that request.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>3. Invoke the provider</h2>
    <p>
      Only after ALLOW may the enterprise application invoke the AI provider for
      that governed request. BeaconGuard does not become the model host or
      replace the customer's chosen provider.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>4. Receive an untrusted result</h2>
    <p>
      The AI-generated result—including any tool or action proposal—is an
      untrusted proposal. It does not confer enterprise authority. Using the AI
      response itself as authorization is not supported.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>5. Authorize the intended action</h2>
    <p>
      The enterprise application declares the intended enterprise action.
      Result-to-Action Authorization is independent authorization of that
      declared action under the Active Verified Release—not semantic scoring of
      response content. DENY prevents enterprise effect. ALLOW authorizes the
      enterprise application to execute the action.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Independent authorization uses a new governed request where required.</li>
      <li>Approving a proposal does not resume the original AI response.</li>
      <li>BeaconGuard authorizes; the enterprise application executes.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>6. Record and verify</h2>
    <p>
      Each authorization decision produces a Verifiable Decision Record.
      BeaconGuard Verify provides read-only investigation through reporting,
      Decision Explorer, deterministic reconstruction, source evidence, replay
      verification, and verifiable export.
    </p>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard Verify is read-only. It cannot modify evidence, change a
      historical decision, activate a release, alter a Compliance Playbook,
      grant runtime authority, or authorize an enterprise action.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Optional System Connector</h2>
    <p>
      Some enterprise applications do not natively produce the protocol or
      context structure required for governed authorization. The optional System
      Connector maps source protocols and context into the required request
      format. It does not evaluate controls, activate releases, or bypass
      enforcement.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Deterministic outcomes</h2>
    <h3>ALLOW</h3>
    <p>
      The governed request satisfies Active Verified Release requirements for
      that authorization point. After evidence persistence where required,
      provider invocation or enterprise execution may proceed as authorized.
    </p>
    <h3>DENY</h3>
    <p>
      The request or declared action is blocked. BeaconGuard fails closed when
      required validation, trust, freshness, replay, release, or control
      conditions are not satisfied. Review does not convert DENY into ALLOW.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Technical deepening</h2>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/platform">Review Platform</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/verification">Explore Verification</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/docs/architecture">System Architecture</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/docs/enforcement-runtime">Enforcement Runtime</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/deployment">Deployment Expectations</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
